          The <a href="/nordictrack-c-1650-treadmill">C 1650</a> is the most
          direct comparison, since it shares the C 990's 3.0 CHP motor and
          0-10% incline range but adds five inches of deck length and a
          larger 10-inch touchscreen for roughly $300 more. If you run
          regularly rather than only walk, that extra deck length is worth
          the difference. The
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          is a useful reference point for how much more machine your money
          buys further up NordicTrack's lineup — a stronger 3.75 CHP motor,
          a larger 22 x 60-inch deck, and a considerably bigger touchscreen,
          for roughly $800 more than the C 990.
        </p>
        <p>
          The gap between the C 990 and the Commercial 1750 looks large on
          paper, and it is — but it's worth being clear about what that gap
          actually buys. For a household that walks regularly and jogs
          occasionally, the C 990 handles that use case competently and
          without the compromises that would matter for a serious runner.
          The extra money for a Commercial-series machine buys headroom you
          may never actually use if your training never pushes past
          moderate jogging speeds.
