        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          As much as we like the C 990 for its intended use case, it isn't
          the right machine for every shopper who lands on this page. If
          you're a regular runner logging miles at faster paces, the shorter
          deck and smaller motor will start to feel limiting sooner than
          you'd like, and the
          <a href="/nordictrack-c-1650-treadmill">C 1650</a> or
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          are worth the extra investment. If you're tall, above roughly 6
          feet, and plan to run rather than walk, the same advice applies
          even more strongly. And if you know for certain you'll never pay
          for an iFit subscription, you're still buying into hardware built
          around that ecosystem, so weigh whether a simpler non-connected
          treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews">budget treadmill guide</a>
          might suit you better.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The NordicTrack C 990 is an honest entry-level treadmill. It
          doesn't pretend to be a Commercial-series machine at a
          Commercial-series price, and the trade-offs — a smaller deck, a
          less powerful motor, a more modest screen — are exactly where
          you'd expect them to be for the money. For walkers and casual
          joggers with a limited budget or limited space, it delivers a
          genuinely solid NordicTrack and iFit experience without
          overselling what it is.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're weighing the C 990 against pricier options, our advice
          is to be honest with yourself about how you'll actually use it.
          If your typical session is a brisk incline walk or an easy jog,
          this machine will serve you well for years. If you already know
          you'll be pushing faster paces or longer runs, spend the extra
          money now on a longer deck rather than discovering the limitation
          the hard way after a few months of ownership.
        </p>
